{
	title: '/recap: a short summary of where a session stands',
	spec: `Like Claude Code's session recap (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/interactive-mode, Session recap). /recap shows a summary of at most 400 characters of what happened in the session so far and where it stands: the goal, what got done, what it waits on. /recap <tab, name or id> recaps another session instead, and /recap all gives one line per other open tab; neither sends that session anything or pauses it. The recap is a model call on the session's recent records (bounded), run in the background so the session stays usable, and is stored as the command's output.
Automatic recap: when the user comes back to a tab (a client focuses it, or the terminal or browser window regains focus) after at least 3 minutes since its last finished turn, the session has at least 3 turns, and the last thing shown there is not already a recap, Hal shows one, generated in the background while the user was away so it's ready on return. A boolean setting sessionRecap (default on, in /config) turns it off. Never in subagent sessions, which have no human watching. Old Hal reference for reading other sessions: artifact old-what.ts.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Recaps reuse command output and the visibility protocol already sent by terminal and web, including window focus. A bounded recent-history digest (80 records, 24,000 characters) goes to the session model without tools; output is normalized to one line and capped at 400 Unicode characters. Other-session summaries are saved only in the requesting session. Automatic generation starts on leaving a tab or a turn ending unwatched, is cached by latest history record, and validates unchanged history before delivery. Background work is cancelled and invalidated on host reset; provider message and body reach durable error output. Turn eligibility scans sliced history pages only until three turn ends. Synthetic offline models are excluded from automatic paid calls.',
		'Manual QA used headless Chromium at 390px and 1280px and terminal frames at 40 and 100 columns: a long recap wraps, no horizontal page overflow, and the composer remains reachable. Both clients use existing output-card styling; no UI-specific implementation or new colors. Mocked provider output checks layout rather than nondeterministic model wording.',
		'Host transcript fixtures disable automatic background recap calls unless a recap test opts in, like their existing naming/greeting isolation. Otherwise a third scripted fake-provider turn correctly attempts a speculative recap and appends an unknown-provider error, polluting unrelated exact-transcript assertions.',
		'Visibility is untrusted: the host rejects a report unless it names a followed session or known open tab (whose snapshot may still be pending). This prevents arbitrary visibility ids from triggering recap disk reads or cascading error writes.'
	],
	needs: []
}
